{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788365404,"nonce":"4kdoIJM86PcCoDS1EVLK+HNPTSQ=","title":"A transcript's step order is CLAIM order, not completion order, and nothing on disk says so","message":"Found while building the cockpit (df378fa) against run `m1-20260901`.\n\n**The measurement.** Across the 4,599-step chain, **141 adjacent step pairs\nhave their `result.ts` inverted** — step N+1's result came back before step N's.\n80 of those are sub-millisecond and are clock granularity. **61 are inverted by\nmore than a second, up to 111 seconds**, and every one of the ten largest has an\n`advance` as the earlier step:\n\n```\n111.1s  -s00/521-advance      -\u003e -s00/522-pawn\n 73.6s  -s03/587-advance      -\u003e -s03/588-letter-dismiss\n 64.2s  -s01/916-advance      -\u003e -s01/917-things\n 50.5s  -s00/468-advance      -\u003e -s00/469-things\n 43.1s  432-advance           -\u003e 433-work-priorities\n```\n\n`-s00/521-advance` ends at tick 2,276,023 with `ts` 04:04:50; `522-pawn`\nreports tick 2,223,181 with `ts` 04:02:59.\n\n**The mechanism.** `rwa` claims the `NNN-\u003cop\u003e/` directory when the command is\nSENT (`Transcript.step` — mkdir is the lock), and writes `cmd.json` before\ndispatch. So the number in a step directory orders the SENDS. Two clients\nagainst one bench interleave their claims, and a 120-second `advance` is\novertaken by a verb that answered instantly. The tick column then reads\nbackwards, which is how this surfaced.\n\n**Why it matters beyond a cosmetic ordering.** `accept/4.2-play-loop.py`'s\n`advance_discipline` reasons about adjacency — \"the very next command\" after an\nadvance is what distinguishes riding past a casualty halt from responding to\none. Where the run interleaved, `steps[i+1]` is not the loop's response to\n`steps[i]`; it is a different client's command that happened to be claimed next.\n**78 of the run's 489 advances have a next step whose result returned earlier**\n(61 of them by more than a second). The grader currently cannot tell those apart\nfrom a genuine re-advance, so a compliance verdict on a multi-client run is\nreporting on an adjacency that did not happen.\n\nThis is the same underlying condition as the shared-watermark hazard\n`Journal.cs` documents and as the duplicate-driver deadlock that froze this run\ntwice — a second client on one bench — but it is a distinct consequence, and\nunlike the watermark it leaves no field anywhere saying it happened. The\ncockpit works around it locally (day anchors take a strict maximum; the journal\ndelta is keyed on `ts` order rather than step order; the sidebar prints\n\"returned BEFORE the previous step\"), but that is one consumer patching around\na property the format does not publish.\n\n**Acceptance**\n\n- A consumer can determine, from the transcript alone, whether two steps were\n  in flight at the same time — without reconstructing it from `ts` inversions.\n  Either the client stamps the step (a `sent`/`returned` pair, or a client\n  instance id in `meta.json` and per-step), or `log.ndjson` gains the send time\n  alongside the existing `elapsed_s`, and `rwa/README.md` states that step\n  order is claim order.\n- `accept/4.2-play-loop.py`'s adjacency rules either consume that signal or\n  state in their output that adjacency is unreliable on the run being graded,\n  naming the count. A green `advance-discipline` line over an interleaved run\n  is the failure mode this is filed against.\n- Verified against `m1-20260901`: the 61 \u003e1s inversions are reported rather\n  than silently ordered.\n\n**Not in scope.** Preventing two clients. That is `65e7cf9` (client liveness),\nand this issue is about the transcript being honest when it happens, not about\nstopping it.","files":null}]}